    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<div style="border-left:4px solid #2c3e50; padding:0.5em 1em; margin-bottom:1em; background:#eef1f4;"><b>Goal</b><p>A lido is a public outdoor swimming pool. Its bylaw admits swimmers from age 8, so the attendant refuses anyone younger, and the result shows lane <code>0</code> and <code>false</code>. An admitted swimmer with a pace above 40 goes to lane 1, and every other admitted swimmer goes to lane 2. Make every return in the lane allocator carry its values.</p></div>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b8860b; padding:0.5em 1em; margin-bottom:1em; background:#fdf8ec;"><b>Read</b><ul><li><a href="https://go.dev/ref/spec#Return_statements">Return statements</a>. Find the third way to return values. What can the function do with named results before returning them? Find the sentence beginning "Regardless of how they are declared". What value does each result hold when the function starts?</li></ul></div>
<details><summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold; padding:0.3em 0;">Coming from Java</summary><p>Java rejects a read of a local variable before assignment, and a method returns one unnamed value. A Java solution therefore declares one temporary for each result, assigns every branch, and returns both values at the end.</p><pre>int lane = 0;
boolean admitted = false;
if (age >= 8) {
    admitted = true;
    lane = pace > 40 ? 1 : 2;
}
return new Allocation(lane, admitted);</pre></details>
